@ArveVM This is not yet possible. The grouped energy sensor powercalc is actually a kind of aggregation of sub energy sensors. So when you don't let powercalc create the individual energy sensors it will return "unknown".

I have to restructure a lot of code (and cause new potential bugs) to only create a integration sensor of the group power sensors, so I won't refactor this on short term. I see it can be useful as most people are not interested in knowing the kWh of each individual light bulb, but for the whole house or particular room.

When you want a single energy sensor now only for the group you can use the Riemann integral to integrate the group power sensor as you already correctly indicated as a solution.

Thanks for sharing the single-panel measurements. We can use them together with the 17-panel run to build one Canvas profile with a configurable panel count. That gives us what we need for a first estimate, so you can leave the wall-mounted panels in place.

For each brightness and colour setting, we’d subtract the one-panel reading from the 17-panel reading and divide by 16. That gives us an estimate of the power each additional panel draws at that setting. The white-temperature runs used slightly different measurement grids, so we’d align those before calculating the difference.

PowerCalc can then multiply the resulting lookup table by the configured panel count and add about 1.1 W once for the fixed overhead while the lights are on. Standby would use its own calculation, based on the 1.6 W and 4.7 W readings. The profile could look like this:

```json
{
  "min_version": "v1.26.0",
  "fields": {
    "panel_count": {
      "label": "Number of Canvas panels (including the control square)",
      "selector": {"number": {"min": 1, "max": 17, "step": 1}}
    }
  },
  "multiply_factor": "[[panel_count]]",
  "standby_power_on": 1.1,
  "standby_power": "{{ 1.6 + ([[panel_count]] - 1) * 0.19375 }}"
}
```

I’d make the panel count required during setup. Discovery can then offer the profile without assuming everyone has 17 panels, which could give a very wrong estimate for a smaller setup. We can document that counts between 1 and 17 are estimates based on your two measurements with a single power supply. Could you also update the PR description to clarify that the 17 panels include the control square and link both measurement runs?
